1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,040
Tuesday, eleven in the morning.

2
00:00:02,090 --> 00:00:07,152
A bouquet ordered and paid at nine arrives wilted, and the buyer asks for the money back.

3
00:00:07,202 --> 00:00:11,400
Malika is happy to give it, as long as nothing goes wrong on the way.

4
00:00:11,450 --> 00:00:23,448
In Lesson 2, a payment cancelled the same day was reversed and a settled one was refunded, and in Lesson 27, one caller won the charge and the logbook was written before the card zone was asked.

5
00:00:23,498 --> 00:00:27,048
This lesson gives money back the same careful way.

6
00:00:27,098 --> 00:00:29,688
Here is what goes wrong without care.

7
00:00:29,738 --> 00:00:32,016
Malika presses give back twice.

8
00:00:32,066 --> 00:00:36,288
The bank's support team refunds the same payment again over the phone.

9
00:00:36,338 --> 00:00:40,656
A refund made six months later reaches a card that no longer exists.

10
00:00:40,706 --> 00:00:43,512
Each mistake pays out too much, or too late.

11
00:00:43,562 --> 00:00:45,432
The first rule depends on the day.

12
00:00:45,482 --> 00:00:50,160
Before the day closes, the payment is simply reversed, and it costs nothing.

13
00:00:50,210 --> 00:00:54,240
After the day closes, the money travels back as a refund.

14
00:00:54,290 --> 00:00:56,976
The day is counted in the bank's own time zone.

15
00:00:57,026 --> 00:01:00,096
And the whole amount always goes back, never a part.

16
00:01:00,146 --> 00:01:04,704
Malika can give back a paid payment by herself, but only up to the refund cap.

17
00:01:04,754 --> 00:01:09,168
That is the bank's chosen largest amount a merchant may give back on its own.

18
00:01:09,218 --> 00:01:13,392
A later refund must also fall inside the refund window.

19
00:01:13,442 --> 00:01:17,280
That is the bank's chosen number of working days after the payment.

20
00:01:17,330 --> 00:01:21,072
Two bank admins together can make one website's window longer.

21
00:01:21,122 --> 00:01:24,720
Inside these limits, giving back works like the charge.

22
00:01:24,770 --> 00:01:28,968
One claim lets only one caller win, however many clicks arrive.

23
00:01:29,018 --> 00:01:33,288
A line is written in the bank's logbook first, with the merchant's reason.

24
00:01:33,338 --> 00:01:37,728
Then ecom asks the card zone, by token, with the call's own id.

25
00:01:37,778 --> 00:01:39,600
Approved is final.

26
00:01:39,650 --> 00:01:44,496
Refused leaves the payment paid, and the merchant is told the refund failed.

27
00:01:44,546 --> 00:01:50,712
No answer is asked about later, the same way as a silent charge, and never sent twice.

28
00:01:50,762 --> 00:01:55,896
Beyond the cap, or after the window, the merchant cannot give money back alone.

29
00:01:55,946 --> 00:01:57,768
The bank does it instead.

30
00:01:57,818 --> 00:01:59,856
That is a refund by application.

31
00:01:59,906 --> 00:02:01,728
The merchant writes to the bank.

32
00:02:01,778 --> 00:02:05,760
One bank admin asks for the refund, and a second admin approves it.

33
00:02:05,810 --> 00:02:12,720
I think this is where a payments product shows its quality, not in how it takes money, but in how carefully it gives money back.

34
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:14,376
Back to the wilted bouquet.

35
00:02:14,426 --> 00:02:17,952
In the merchant portal, Malika opens the paid payment.

36
00:02:18,002 --> 00:02:22,968
A box says refundable until, with the date, and how many days are left.

37
00:02:23,018 --> 00:02:24,840
She presses give back.

38
00:02:24,890 --> 00:02:27,264
A window asks: give the money back?

39
00:02:27,314 --> 00:02:32,592
It explains that the whole amount goes back to the buyer's card, reversed today, or refunded later.

40
00:02:32,642 --> 00:02:36,600
It asks for a reason, which both Malika and the bank will see.

41
00:02:36,650 --> 00:02:38,472
She confirms.

42
00:02:38,522 --> 00:02:40,944
The status turns to giving the money back.

43
00:02:40,994 --> 00:02:45,024
Moments later it says reversed, because it is still the same day.

44
00:02:45,074 --> 00:02:49,464
Under calls to the card scheme, a reversal line appears below the debit.

45
00:02:49,514 --> 00:02:52,416
On the buyer's page, it says money returned.

46
00:02:52,466 --> 00:02:56,328
Now Malika opens a hotel's large order, above the cap.

47
00:02:56,378 --> 00:02:58,200
There is no give back button.

48
00:02:58,250 --> 00:03:05,112
A grey note says above the refund limit, and that larger refunds are made by the bank on a written application.

49
00:03:05,162 --> 00:03:07,464
She opens an old payment from the spring.

50
00:03:07,514 --> 00:03:13,080
The note says the refund window has closed, and gives the date it could be refunded until.

51
00:03:13,130 --> 00:03:16,416
A teammate who only views sees no refund box at all.

52
00:03:16,466 --> 00:03:18,264
So Malika writes to the bank.

53
00:03:18,314 --> 00:03:23,952
In the admin portal, Dilnoza opens that old payment and chooses refund by application.

54
00:03:24,002 --> 00:03:27,192
She enters the application number and a note saying why.

55
00:03:27,242 --> 00:03:31,992
She presses send for approval, and the screen says it was sent to another admin.

56
00:03:32,042 --> 00:03:35,616
Only when a second admin approves does the money go back.

57
00:03:35,666 --> 00:03:37,896
So the claim stops double refunds.

58
00:03:37,946 --> 00:03:41,256
The logbook means every refund can be explained.

59
00:03:41,306 --> 00:03:45,696
The cap and the window protect Malika from large or late mistakes.

60
00:03:45,746 --> 00:03:49,776
And two admins protect the bank from one person moving money alone.

61
00:03:49,826 --> 00:03:52,992
Next: following one payment through the whole system.
